## What is React?

React is a JavaScript library for building user interfaces. It is especially popular for applications where the page changes frequently based on user actions or data.

~~~diagram
{"type": "tree", "title": "A screen is a tree of components", "caption": "Each box is a function that returns JSX. Small, named pieces are easier to read, reuse and test.", "root": {"label": "<App>", "highlight": true, "children": [{"label": "<Header>", "children": [{"label": "<Logo>"}, {"label": "<Nav>"}]}, {"label": "<TaskPage>", "highlight": true, "children": [{"label": "<TaskForm>"}, {"label": "<TaskList>", "children": [{"label": "<TaskItem>"}, {"label": "<TaskItem>"}]}]}, {"label": "<Footer>"}]}}
~~~

Instead of thinking about a page as one large piece of HTML, React encourages you to split the interface into **components**. A component is a reusable piece of UI with its own structure and behaviour.

Imagine a task management application. You might have components such as:

- `Header`
- `TaskList`
- `TaskItem`
- `UserMenu`
- `Button`

Each component can be developed and understood separately. You can then combine these smaller pieces into a complete application.

This approach becomes particularly useful as an application grows. A 2,000-line component is difficult to understand, while ten smaller components with clear responsibilities are much easier to work with.

---

## Creating a React app with Vite

**Vite** is a modern frontend build tool and development server. It gives you a fast development environment for React and other frontend technologies.

You can create a new React application from a terminal with:

~~~text
npm create vite@latest
~~~

The command starts an interactive setup. You can choose a project name, select React, and then select JavaScript or TypeScript depending on what you want to use.

For example, a typical setup can look like:

~~~text
npm create vite@latest my-react-app
~~~

Then enter the project directory and install its dependencies:

~~~text
cd my-react-app
npm install
npm run dev
~~~

Vite prints a local development URL, usually something similar to `http://localhost:5173`.

Open the project folder in **IntelliJ IDEA**. IntelliJ IDEA can provide syntax highlighting, autocomplete, navigation, refactoring support, and error highlighting for a React project.

A Vite React project contains files such as `package.json`, `index.html`, and a `src` directory. The exact generated structure can change between versions, so focus on the role of the files rather than memorising every generated file.

---

## Your first component

A React function component is simply a JavaScript function that returns UI.

~~~jsx
function Welcome() {
  return <h1>Hello!</h1>;
}
~~~

The function name starts with a capital letter. This is important because React treats lowercase names as HTML elements and capitalised names as components.

You can use the component inside another component:

~~~jsx
function App() {
  return <Welcome />;
}
~~~

`<Welcome />` is JSX syntax for rendering the `Welcome` component.

Components do not have to be large. In fact, a small component with one clear responsibility is often easier to reuse and maintain.

---

## JSX

**JSX** is a syntax extension that lets you write markup-like code directly inside JavaScript or TypeScript.

~~~compare
{"left": {"title": "JSX you write", "lang": "jsx", "code": "<h1 className=\"title\">\n  Hello, {name}!\n</h1>", "note": "Looks like HTML, but it is JavaScript: className, {expressions}."}, "right": {"title": "What it becomes", "lang": "js", "code": "React.createElement(\n  'h1',\n  { className: 'title' },\n  'Hello, ', name, '!'\n);", "note": "The build tool (Vite) compiles JSX into plain function calls."}}
~~~

For example:

~~~jsx
const title = "My tasks";

function Header() {
  return <h1>{title}</h1>;
}
~~~

JSX may look like HTML, but it is not exactly HTML. It is transformed into JavaScript by the build process.

There are several rules you need to know.

### One root element

A component must return one JSX tree. This means you cannot normally return two sibling elements directly:

~~~jsx
function Example() {
  return (
    <h1>Hello</h1>
    <p>Welcome</p>
  );
}
~~~

Instead, wrap them in one parent:

~~~jsx
function Example() {
  return (
    <div>
      <h1>Hello</h1>
      <p>Welcome</p>
    </div>
  );
}
~~~

You can also use a **fragment**, which groups elements without adding an extra DOM element:

~~~jsx
function Example() {
  return (
    <>
      <h1>Hello</h1>
      <p>Welcome</p>
    </>
  );
}
~~~

Fragments are useful when you do not need an additional `<div>` in the resulting HTML.

### `className` instead of `class`

JSX uses `className` for CSS classes:

~~~jsx
<div className="card">Hello</div>
~~~

Instead of:

~~~jsx
<div class="card">Hello</div>
~~~

### CamelCase attributes

Many JSX attributes use camelCase because they correspond to JavaScript properties.

For example:

~~~jsx
<button onClick={handleClick}>Save</button>
<input onChange={handleChange} />
~~~

Notice `onClick` rather than `onclick`.

### Close elements

JSX requires elements to be properly closed.

~~~jsx
<img src="photo.jpg" alt="Profile" />
<input type="text" />
~~~

A normal element needs both an opening and closing tag:

~~~jsx
<p>Hello</p>
~~~

### JavaScript expressions use braces

Use `{}` when you want to insert a JavaScript expression into JSX.

~~~jsx
const name = "Anna";

function Greeting() {
  return <h2>Hello, {name}!</h2>;
}
~~~

You can also use expressions such as calculations or function calls:

~~~jsx
<p>Total: {price * quantity}</p>
<p>{name.toUpperCase()}</p>
~~~

Statements such as a normal `if` block cannot be placed directly inside JSX, but expressions such as ternaries and logical `&&` can be used for conditional rendering.

---

## Props

**Props**, short for properties, are values passed from a parent component to a child component.

Imagine a reusable profile card:

~~~jsx
function ProfileCard({ name, role }) {
  return (
    <article>
      <h2>{name}</h2>
      <p>{role}</p>
    </article>
  );
}
~~~

The parent can provide different values:

~~~jsx
function App() {
  return (
    <>
      <ProfileCard name="Anna" role="Frontend developer" />
      <ProfileCard name="Bence" role="Backend developer" />
    </>
  );
}
~~~

The same component now renders two different profiles.

The `{ name, role }` syntax in the function parameter is **destructuring**. It extracts those values from the props object.

You can also access the complete props object:

~~~jsx
function ProfileCard(props) {
  return <h2>{props.name}</h2>;
}
~~~

Both approaches are valid. Destructuring is often convenient when a component has a small number of clearly named props.

---

## Rendering lists with `map`

Applications often need to display a list of data. JavaScript's `map` method is commonly used for this.

~~~callout warn Keys must be stable
Use an id from your data as `key`, not the array index. With index keys, deleting or sorting items can make React reuse the wrong component — inputs keep the old text and checkboxes jump.
~~~

For example:

~~~jsx
const skills = ["React", "CSS", "JavaScript"];

function Skills() {
  return (
    <ul>
      {skills.map(skill => (
        <li key={skill}>{skill}</li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  );
}
~~~

The `map` method creates one `<li>` for every item in the array.

The `key` is important. React uses keys to identify individual items in a list between renders.

For example, imagine a list of tasks where a new task is inserted at the beginning. Stable keys help React understand which existing task is which instead of treating the list as unrelated elements.

A key should normally come from a stable identifier in your data:

~~~jsx
const tasks = [
  { id: 101, title: "Learn React" },
  { id: 102, title: "Build an app" }
];

function TaskList() {
  return (
    <ul>
      {tasks.map(task => (
        <li key={task.id}>{task.title}</li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  );
}
~~~

Avoid using the array index as a key when items can be inserted, removed, or reordered. A stable ID is usually a better choice.

---

## Conditional rendering

React applications frequently need to show different UI depending on data.

One common technique is `&&`.

~~~jsx
function UserMenu({ isAdmin }) {
  return (
    <div>
      <span>Profile</span>
      {isAdmin && <button>Admin panel</button>}
    </div>
  );
}
~~~

If `isAdmin` is truthy, React renders the button. Otherwise, it renders nothing for that expression.

For two alternatives, a **ternary expression** is often useful:

~~~jsx
function Status({ completed }) {
  return <p>{completed ? "Completed" : "Not completed"}</p>;
}
~~~

You can also conditionally render larger pieces of JSX:

~~~jsx
function Skills({ skills }) {
  return skills.length > 0 ? (
    <ul>
      {skills.map(skill => <li key={skill}>{skill}</li>)}
    </ul>
  ) : (
    <p>No skills yet</p>
  );
}
~~~

This pattern is extremely common in real applications because data is rarely complete all the time. A component may need to show a loading state, an empty state, an error, or the actual content.

---

## Composing components

**Composition** means building a larger interface by combining smaller components.

For example:

~~~jsx
function Header() {
  return <header>My application</header>;
}

function TaskList() {
  return <main>Tasks go here</main>;
}

function Footer() {
  return <footer>Copyright</footer>;
}

function App() {
  return (
    <>
      <Header />
      <TaskList />
      <Footer />
    </>
  );
}
~~~

`App` does not need to know every implementation detail of the individual components. It simply combines them.

This is one of the central ideas behind React: **build small components and compose them into larger interfaces**.

Later, you will also learn how components can receive children, manage state, perform effects, and communicate with other parts of an application.

---

## A practical file structure

A small React application might start with a structure like this:

~~~text
src/
  components/
    Header.jsx
    ProfileCard.jsx
    TaskList.jsx
  App.jsx
  main.jsx
  index.css
~~~

The exact structure is a project decision, not a React requirement.

`main.jsx` is commonly responsible for starting the React application and rendering the root component.

`App.jsx` often acts as the main application component.

A `components` directory can contain reusable UI components.

For example, `ProfileCard.jsx` might contain:

~~~jsx
function ProfileCard({ name, role }) {
  return (
    <article className="profile-card">
      <h2>{name}</h2>
      <p>{role}</p>
    </article>
  );
}

export default ProfileCard;
~~~

Then another file can import it:

~~~jsx
import ProfileCard from "./components/ProfileCard";

function App() {
  return <ProfileCard name="Anna" role="Frontend developer" />;
}

export default App;
~~~

These imports are normal JavaScript module imports. The exercise in this lesson is simplified so it can run directly in the course editor without requiring imports.

---

## Common mistakes

### Using lowercase component names

`<profileCard />` is not treated like your custom React component. Use a capitalised name such as `<ProfileCard />`.

### Forgetting `className`

JSX uses `className`, not `class`, for CSS classes.

### Forgetting keys in lists

Elements created with `map` should have a stable `key`. Prefer an ID from your data rather than an array index when the list can change.

### Returning multiple root elements

A component needs one root JSX tree. Use a parent element or a fragment when you need several sibling elements.

### Treating JSX as ordinary HTML

JSX follows JavaScript-oriented rules. Attributes such as `onClick` use camelCase, and JavaScript expressions go inside `{}`.

### Making one huge component

If a component contains unrelated sections and becomes difficult to understand, consider splitting it into smaller components. Components are a tool for managing complexity, not a requirement to create a separate component for every single element.

---

## The bigger picture

React becomes much easier to understand once you see the basic pattern: **data goes into components through props, and components return UI through JSX**.

Arrays can become lists with `map`. Conditional expressions can decide which UI is displayed. Components can be composed into larger components, allowing a complete application to be built from smaller, understandable pieces.

In the next React lessons, these components will become interactive. You will learn how React stores changing data with state, responds to user actions, and updates the interface when that data changes.

